>{> If you want to identify real gems from worthless pieces of glass here is
>{> what you do.  You find a convenient chest/box/etc (i think a bag works
>{> as well but I'm not positive) and dump all your gems into it.  Then you
>{> sit there and happily kick it a dozen times.  Note: the muffled shatters
>{> you might hear are the worthless pieces of glass shattering!  When
>{> you're satisfied that all the worthless ones are destroyed get the rest
>{> out and they are all worth _soemthing_.
>{> 
>
>i good idea after you identify the "unbroken" gens is to name them, that 
>way you won't get confused later on when you start picking up new gens
>
Suppose you use this method to find "good" gems.  Can you sell these
gems for their real worth rather than 3 or 4$?  You haven't identified
them, (garnet stone, diamond, etc.), you just know that they are
valuable.  Will a shopkeeper still try to rip you off?  If so, my
point about A's stands; if not an A, you will still have to identify
them (scroll or spell) to be able to get the full value when selling.
If this is not the case (i.e., if non-A's don't have to identify gems
in order to get market value) then I see no real advantage for A's
(other than they start with intrinsic speed, but so do S's, and S's
usually have better initial stats).


